#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gen_n_fill.py -- add programming/algorithms pairs to upgrade_n.jsonl"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades')
OUT.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

pairs = [
("What is time complexity and how do you analyze it?","Time complexity measures how running time grows with input size n. Big O notation: O(1) constant (hash lookup); O(log n) logarithmic (binary search -- halves search space each step); O(n) linear (single loop over n elements); O(n log n) linearithmic (merge sort, heap sort); O(n^2) quadratic (nested loops, bubble sort); O(2^n) exponential (brute-force subset enumeration); O(n!) factorial (permutation generation). Analysis: count dominant operations; drop constants and lower-order terms (5n^2 + 3n + 2 = O(n^2)); worst case is default unless stated. Example: binary search -- each comparison halves n; after k steps n/2^k = 1; k = log2(n) = O(log n). Amortized: dynamic array append is O(1) amortized even though occasional resize is O(n) because resize doubles array and cost is spread across n appends."),
("Explain merge sort and why it is O(n log n).","Merge sort: divide array in half recursively until single elements; merge sorted halves. def merge_sort(arr): if len(arr)<=1: return arr; mid=len(arr)//2; left=merge_sort(arr[:mid]); right=merge_sort(arr[mid:]); return merge(left,right). Merge function: two pointers compare elements from each half, place smaller into result -- O(n). Recursion depth: log2(n) levels (halving each time). Each level does O(n) merge work total. Total: O(n) * O(log n) = O(n log n). Space: O(n) for auxiliary arrays. Stable sort (equal elements maintain relative order). Not in-place without modification. Preferred for: linked lists, external sorting, stable sort requirement. Worst, average, best case all O(n log n)."),
("How does quicksort work and what is its time complexity?","Quicksort: choose pivot; partition array so elements less than pivot are left, greater are right; recursively sort each partition. Partition (Lomuto scheme): pivot=arr[high]; i=low-1; for j in range(low,high): if arr[j]<=pivot: i++; swap(arr[i],arr[j]); swap(arr[i+1],arr[high]); return i+1. Time: average O(n log n) -- good pivot splits array roughly in half; worst O(n^2) -- sorted array with last-element pivot causes n-1 and 0 split; best O(n log n). Space: O(log n) call stack average. Optimisations: randomised pivot; median-of-three; introsort (Python/C++ std::sort -- quicksort + heapsort fallback when recursion exceeds 2*log n). In-place, cache-friendly -- faster than merge sort in practice despite same asymptotic complexity."),
("What is a hash table and how does it handle collisions?","Hash table maps keys to values via hash function h(key) = index into backing array. Operations: get/set/delete O(1) average. Collision handling: (1) Chaining -- each bucket holds linked list of (key,value) pairs; O(1) average, O(n) worst; Python dicts use open addressing. (2) Open addressing -- on collision, probe for next empty slot; Linear probing: check index+1, +2, ... (cache-friendly but clustering); Quadratic probing: index+1^2, +2^2; Double hashing: second hash function determines step size. Load factor: n_elements / n_buckets; when load factor exceeds threshold (0.75 in Java HashMap, 2/3 in Python), resize and rehash O(n) amortized O(1). Hash table is the most important data structure -- used for O(1) lookup in sets, maps, caches, databases, deduplication."),
("Explain binary search trees and their operations.","BST property: for every node, all left subtree values less than node, all right values greater. Operations: Search -- compare key with node, go left if smaller, right if larger; O(h) where h=height. Insert -- find correct position by search, insert new node; O(h). Delete -- three cases: leaf (just remove); one child (replace with child); two children (find inorder successor -- smallest in right subtree -- swap value, delete successor). Inorder traversal visits nodes in sorted order O(n). Height h: O(log n) for balanced tree, O(n) worst case for sorted insertion. Self-balancing: AVL tree maintains height difference <= 1 via rotations after insert/delete -- guarantees O(log n); Red-Black tree (C++ std::map, Java TreeMap) relaxed balance via coloring -- O(log n) with less rotation overhead."),
("How does dynamic programming work? Give an example.","Dynamic programming (DP): solve complex problem by breaking into overlapping subproblems; cache results to avoid recomputation. Two approaches: (1) Top-down (memoisation) -- recursive solution plus cache; if subproblem already solved, return cached result. (2) Bottom-up (tabulation) -- solve smallest subproblems first; fill table iteratively; no recursion overhead. Classic -- Fibonacci: naive O(2^n); memo: use dict to cache fib(n) = fib(n-1)+fib(n-2) giving O(n) time O(n) space; bottom-up: track just two variables for O(1) space. Identifying DP: overlapping subproblems (same calculation needed multiple times); optimal substructure (optimal solution built from optimal sub-solutions). Other classics: Longest Common Subsequence O(mn); 0/1 Knapsack O(nW); Coin Change O(amount times coins); Edit Distance O(mn). DP state definition is the hardest part -- define what dp[i] represents precisely."),
("Explain graph algorithms -- BFS, DFS, Dijkstra.","Graph G = (V, E): V vertices, E edges; directed or undirected; weighted or unweighted. BFS (Breadth-First Search): level-by-level traversal using queue; finds shortest path in unweighted graph; O(V+E); use deque: q=deque([src]); visited={src}; while q: node=q.popleft(); for nei in graph[node]: if nei not in visited: visited.add(nei); q.append(nei). DFS (Depth-First Search): explore deep before backtracking; O(V+E); used for cycle detection, topological sort, connected components; recursive: def dfs(node,visited): visited.add(node); for nei in graph[node]: if nei not in visited: dfs(nei,visited). Dijkstra (shortest path, non-negative weights): priority queue; dist[src]=0, others=inf; pop minimum, relax neighbours; O((V+E) log V) with binary heap. Bellman-Ford: handles negative weights O(VE). Floyd-Warshall: all-pairs shortest path O(V^3)."),
("What is a heap data structure and how is it used?","Heap is a complete binary tree satisfying the heap property. Min-heap: parent <= children (root is minimum). Stored as array: parent of index i is at (i-1)//2; children at 2i+1 and 2i+2. Operations: insert O(log n) -- append to end, sift up; extract-min O(log n) -- remove root, move last element to root, sift down; peek O(1). Python heapq: min-heap only; heapq.heappush(h,val); heapq.heappop(h); heapq.heapify(list) O(n). For max-heap negate values. Applications: priority queue (task scheduling); heapsort O(n log n) in-place; k largest/smallest elements; merge k sorted lists; Dijkstra priority queue; median maintenance (two heaps: max-heap for lower half, min-heap for upper half)."),
("Explain object-oriented programming principles.","OOP four pillars: (1) Encapsulation -- bundle data and behaviour inside a class; restrict direct access to internal state; expose controlled interface via public methods; hides implementation, reduces coupling. (2) Inheritance -- child class inherits attributes and methods from parent; enables code reuse; Python: class Dog(Animal): ...; super().__init__() calls parent constructor. (3) Polymorphism -- same interface, different implementations; method overriding (child provides different implementation); duck typing in Python (just needs to implement the interface, no inheritance required). (4) Abstraction -- expose essential features, hide complexity; abstract classes in Python via ABC and abstractmethod; defines contract that subclasses must implement. SOLID principles: Single Responsibility; Open/Closed (open for extension, closed for modification); Liskov Substitution; Interface Segregation; Dependency Inversion."),
("What are design patterns and give examples?","Design patterns are reusable solutions to common software design problems. Creational: Singleton -- only one instance exists; used for DB connections, configuration. Factory Method -- superclass defines creation interface, subclasses decide which class to instantiate. Builder -- construct complex objects step by step; used for query builders. Structural: Adapter -- wrap incompatible interface to make it compatible; legacy code integration. Decorator -- add behaviour dynamically without modifying class; logging, caching, authentication. Observer -- subject notifies observers of state changes; used in event systems, MVC. Strategy -- define family of algorithms, make them interchangeable; swap sorting algorithm at runtime. Composite -- tree structure where leaf and composite nodes share same interface; file system. Behavioral: Command -- encapsulate request as object; enables undo/redo. Iterator -- traverse collection without exposing internal structure. Template Method -- define algorithm skeleton in base class; subclasses override specific steps."),
("Explain recursion and how to avoid stack overflow.","Recursion: function calls itself with smaller input; requires base case and recursive case. Stack frames: each call creates new frame; Python default limit is 1000. Avoiding stack overflow: (1) Convert to iteration with explicit stack: DFS can be done with list as stack instead of call stack -- avoids Python recursion limit for deep graphs. (2) Memoisation reduces depth for DP problems. (3) Increase limit: sys.setrecursionlimit(10000) -- risky but possible. (4) Trampolining -- convert recursive function to return lambdas (thunks); trampoline function calls thunks in loop. Recursion strengths: elegant, matches problem structure (tree/graph traversal, divide and conquer); weaknesses: stack overflow risk, harder to debug. Tree recursion (multiple recursive calls) -- exponential calls without memoisation."),
("What is a linked list and when to use it over an array?","Linked list: nodes containing data and pointer to next node. Operations: insert at head O(1); search O(n) -- must traverse from head; no random access. Array comparison: array gives O(1) random access by index; linked list O(n); array insertion/deletion at middle O(n) (shift elements); linked list O(1) once you have node reference; arrays have better cache locality (contiguous memory). Use linked list when: frequent insertions/deletions at arbitrary positions; implementing stack/queue O(1); LRU cache (doubly linked list plus hash map for O(1) all operations). Use array when: frequent random access; better cache performance. Python list is a dynamic array; collections.deque is doubly linked list. Interview patterns: reverse linked list; detect cycle (Floyd fast/slow pointer); find middle; merge two sorted lists."),
("Explain Python generators and iterators.","Iterator protocol: object implementing __iter__ (returns self) and __next__ (returns next value, raises StopIteration when done). Generators: function using yield keyword; returns generator object which is an iterator; lazy evaluation -- values computed on demand, not all at once; memory efficient. def count_up(n): for i in range(n): yield i. Generator expressions: (x**2 for x in range(1000)) -- lazy, memory O(1) vs list comprehension O(n). send() method: send values into running generator; enables coroutines. yield from: delegate to sub-generator. Practical uses: reading large files line by line; infinite sequences (Fibonacci generator); pipeline processing; cooperative multitasking. Performance: generator is faster when not all values are needed; for full iteration list comprehension slightly faster."),
("What is concurrency vs parallelism in Python?","Concurrency: tasks make progress by interleaving (single core). Parallelism: tasks execute simultaneously (requires multiple cores). Python GIL (Global Interpreter Lock): CPython has GIL -- only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time; prevents true thread parallelism for CPU-bound tasks. Threading (concurrent, not parallel for CPU): good for I/O-bound tasks (HTTP requests, DB queries, file I/O) -- GIL released during I/O; shared memory (need locks for thread safety). asyncio (concurrent, single-threaded): event loop; async def and await; cooperative multitasking; excellent for I/O-bound tasks with many concurrent connections; no GIL issues. Multiprocessing (parallel): separate Python processes each with own GIL; true parallelism for CPU-bound; IPC overhead. Rule of thumb: I/O-bound: asyncio or ThreadPoolExecutor; CPU-bound: ProcessPoolExecutor or numpy (releases GIL for BLAS operations)."),
("Explain REST API design principles.","REST architectural constraints: (1) Stateless -- server holds no client session state; each request contains all info needed; enables horizontal scaling. (2) Resource-based -- everything is a resource with URI; nouns not verbs: /users/123/orders not /getOrdersForUser?id=123. (3) HTTP methods as verbs: GET (read, idempotent, safe); POST (create, not idempotent); PUT (replace, idempotent); PATCH (partial update); DELETE (remove, idempotent). (4) Standard status codes: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 422 Unprocessable Entity, 429 Too Many Requests, 500 Internal Server Error. URL conventions: plural nouns; nested resources for relationships; query params for filtering/sorting/pagination. Error responses: structured JSON with code, message, details. Documentation: OpenAPI/Swagger specification."),
("What are the differences between SQL and NoSQL databases?","SQL (relational): structured data in tables; schema enforced; ACID transactions; powerful JOIN operations. Examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite. NoSQL: flexible schemas; designed for horizontal scaling; eventual consistency often. Types: (1) Document (MongoDB, Firestore) -- JSON-like documents; flexible schema; good for hierarchical data; no joins (denormalise). (2) Key-Value (Redis, DynamoDB) -- O(1) read/write; caching, sessions, leaderboards. (3) Column-family (Cassandra) -- optimised for write-heavy workloads, time-series data; masterless, geo-distributed. (4) Graph (Neo4j) -- nodes and edges; optimal for highly connected data; social networks, fraud detection. When to use SQL: complex relationships, ACID required, complex queries, reporting. When NoSQL: massive scale, flexible schema, specific access patterns, geographic distribution."),
("Explain microservices architecture and its tradeoffs.","Microservices: application built as suite of small, independently deployable services; each service owns its data; communicate via APIs or message queues. Advantages: (1) Independent deployment -- update payment service without touching checkout; faster releases. (2) Technology heterogeneity -- Python for ML, Go for low-latency, Node.js for real-time. (3) Fault isolation -- payment crash does not take down entire application with circuit breakers. (4) Independent scaling. Disadvantages: (1) Distributed systems complexity -- network failures, partial failures, latency; need circuit breakers, retries, timeouts. (2) Data consistency -- no ACID across services; use saga pattern for distributed transactions. (3) Service discovery. (4) Operational overhead -- Kubernetes, service mesh, distributed tracing, centralised logging. When NOT to use: small teams, early-stage startup, unclear domain boundaries; start with monolith and extract services when boundaries become clear."),
("What is memory management in Python?","Python memory management: (1) Reference counting -- primary GC; every object has refcount; incremented on assignment; decremented on deletion; when refcount reaches 0, memory freed immediately; cannot handle reference cycles. (2) Cyclic GC -- supplementary collector handles reference cycles; uses generational collection: generation 0 (new objects, collected frequently), gen 1, gen 2 (old objects, rarely collected); objects surviving a collection promoted to next generation. When to be aware: large object graphs with cycles; __del__ finalizers on cyclic objects complicate collection. Memory profiling: tracemalloc (built-in); memory_profiler; objgraph. CPython: small objects (<=512 bytes) via pymalloc (avoids system allocator overhead); large objects go directly to malloc."),
("Explain the TCP/IP model and how HTTP works.","TCP/IP four-layer model: (1) Application: HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SSH, DNS, SMTP. (2) Transport: TCP (reliable, ordered, connection-oriented) or UDP (unreliable, connectionless, lower overhead). (3) Internet: IP -- addressing and routing across networks. (4) Network access: Ethernet, Wi-Fi -- physical transmission. HTTP request flow: (1) DNS resolution -- browser queries DNS for domain, gets IP. (2) TCP 3-way handshake: SYN then SYN-ACK then ACK. (3) TLS handshake (HTTPS): ClientHello, ServerHello with cert, key exchange (ECDHE), both derive session keys; ~1 RTT with TLS 1.3. (4) HTTP request sent with method, headers, body. (5) Server sends HTTP response with status code, headers, body. (6) Connection reuse (HTTP/1.1 keep-alive) or multiplexing (HTTP/2 multiple requests over single TCP connection). HTTP/3: QUIC (UDP-based -- eliminates TCP head-of-line blocking; built-in TLS 1.3)."),
("What are Python anti-patterns to avoid?","Python anti-patterns: (1) Mutable default argument -- def append(item, lst=[]): lst persists between calls; fix: use lst=None and create list inside. (2) Bare except -- catches everything including KeyboardInterrupt; fix: except Exception: or specific exceptions. (3) Using == for None/True/False -- use is: if x is None. (4) Catching and re-raising badly -- except Exception as e: raise Exception(str(e)) loses traceback; use raise or raise NewException from e. (5) Global state -- creates coupling and makes testing hard; pass dependencies explicitly. (6) String concatenation in loop -- s = s + part each iteration creates new string O(n^2); use list.append() then join(). (7) Shadowing built-ins -- list=[], id=5, type=str breaks built-in functionality. (8) Not using context managers -- open files without with block causes resource leak. (9) import * pollutes namespace. (10) Not using enumerate/zip -- for i in range(len(lst)): instead of for i,v in enumerate(lst)."),
("Explain Git branching strategies.","Git branching strategies: (1) Feature branching (GitHub Flow) -- main is always deployable; create feature branch for every change; open PR; code review; merge to main; deploy immediately; simple, fast release cycle. (2) Gitflow -- main (stable releases); develop (integration branch); feature/ branches off develop; release/ branch for stabilisation; hotfix/ off main for emergency patches; more structured, suits scheduled release software. (3) Trunk-based development -- all developers commit to main or short-lived branches (<1 day); continuous integration; feature flags hide incomplete features; requires high test coverage; preferred by Google, Meta. Key commands: git checkout -b feature/login; git pull origin main --rebase; git rebase -i HEAD~3 (squash commits); git cherry-pick abc123 (apply single commit); git bisect (binary search for bug-introducing commit). Merge vs rebase: merge preserves full history with merge commits; rebase creates linear history (rewrites history -- never rebase shared branches)."),
("What is the difference between authentication and authorization?","Authentication (AuthN): verifying identity -- who are you? Methods: password, OTP/TOTP authenticator app, biometric, hardware key (FIDO2/WebAuthn). Multi-factor combines 2+ categories. Protocols: HTTP Basic (base64 -- insecure without HTTPS); Bearer token (JWT or opaque token); OAuth 2.0 for delegated auth; OIDC adds identity layer on OAuth 2.0 (ID token with user info); SAML for enterprise SSO. Authorization (AuthZ): verifying permissions -- what are you allowed to do? Models: (1) RBAC -- users assigned roles; permissions attached to roles; simple, most common. (2) ABAC -- policy evaluates attributes of user, resource, environment; more flexible. (3) PBAC -- policies defined in policy language (OPA with Rego, AWS IAM JSON). (4) ACL -- per-resource list of who can do what. Implementation: check authorization on every request server-side; never rely on client-side hiding; fail closed (deny by default)."),
("Explain how a database index works.","Database index: auxiliary data structure that speeds up retrieval at cost of write overhead and storage. Without index: full table scan O(n). With B-tree index: O(log n). B-tree: sorted tree with multiple keys per node; balanced; height ~log_k(n) where k is branching factor (~100-1000 for disk pages); leaf pages linked for range scans. How SELECT uses index: planner checks if index exists; traverse B-tree to find matching leaf pages O(log n); follow pointers to heap pages to fetch full rows. Covering index: index includes all columns needed by query -- no heap access: CREATE INDEX idx ON orders(user_id, status); SELECT status WHERE user_id=123 uses index only. Index types: B-tree (default, range and equality); Hash (equality only); GIN (array, JSONB, full-text search). Index selectivity: high selectivity (user_id) = effective; low selectivity (boolean) = full scan often faster. Composite index column order matters: (a,b) index helps WHERE a=x AND b=y and WHERE a=x but NOT WHERE b=y alone."),
("What is SOLID and how do you apply it?","SOLID five OOP design principles: (1) Single Responsibility -- class has one reason to change; separate UserRepository (data), EmailService (notifications), ReportGenerator (PDFs) rather than one giant UserService. (2) Open/Closed -- open for extension, closed for modification; add behaviour via inheritance or composition, not by editing existing code; Shape base class with abstract area(); each shape subclass implements area(); add new shape = new class. (3) Liskov Substitution -- subclass must be substitutable for parent without altering correctness; Square as Rectangle subclass violates LSP because setting width must also set height (breaks Rectangle contract). (4) Interface Segregation -- clients should not depend on interfaces they do not use; split fat Worker interface into Workable and Feedable; RobotWorker implements only Workable. (5) Dependency Inversion -- high-level modules depend on abstractions not concretions; OrderService depends on DatabaseInterface; inject concrete MySQLDatabase at runtime; enables testing with MockDatabase."),
("Explain stack vs queue and when to use each.","Stack: Last In First Out (LIFO); operations: push, pop, peek; O(1) all. Uses: function call stack; undo/redo; balanced parentheses checking; DFS iterative; expression evaluation; backtracking. Python: use list (list.append()=push, list.pop()=pop from right). Queue: First In First Out (FIFO); operations: enqueue, dequeue, peek; O(1) with deque. Uses: BFS; task scheduling; message queues; web server request handling; rate limiting. Python: collections.deque (doubly-linked list -- O(1) append and popleft vs list which is O(n) for pop from front). Priority queue: elements dequeued by priority; heap implementation; Python: heapq or queue.PriorityQueue (thread-safe); used in Dijkstra, A*, task schedulers. Deque (double-ended queue): O(1) add/remove from both ends; sliding window maximum (monotonic deque); LRU cache."),
("How does virtual memory work?","Virtual memory: each process gets illusion of its own private address space. Pages and page tables: memory divided into fixed-size pages (typically 4KB); virtual address = page number + offset; page table maps virtual page numbers to physical frame numbers; multi-level page tables (x86-64 uses 4 levels). TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer): CPU cache for recent page table entries; virtual-to-physical translation in 1 cycle (TLB hit) vs many cycles (page table walk); TLB flushed on context switch. Page fault: process accesses unmapped virtual address; OS handles: if valid but not in RAM (swapped out): load from swap into physical frame; update page table; resume. If invalid: SIGSEGV. Memory-mapped files: file treated as memory region; page faults load portions from disk on access; used by databases, executables. Copy-on-Write: fork() creates child sharing parent pages read-only; on write, page is copied; efficient for fork-exec."),
("Explain common sorting algorithms.","Bubble sort O(n^2) average/worst; stable; in-place; avoid in production. Insertion sort O(n^2) average, O(n) best; stable; efficient for nearly sorted data and small arrays; used as base case in introsort. Selection sort O(n^2) all cases; unstable; minimises writes. Merge sort O(n log n) all cases; stable; O(n) extra space; preferred for linked lists, external sorting, stable sort. Heap sort O(n log n) all cases; unstable; in-place; guaranteed O(n log n) without extra space. Quicksort O(n log n) average, O(n^2) worst; unstable; fastest in practice due to cache efficiency. Timsort (Python default): hybrid merge + insertion sort; O(n log n) worst, O(n) best (already sorted); stable; adapts to existing order. Counting sort O(n+k); non-comparison; for integers in small range. Rule: use Python sorted()/list.sort() (Timsort) for general purpose; counting/radix for integer keys; merge sort when stability and guaranteed O(n log n) matter."),
("What are Python decorators and how do they work?","Decorator: function that takes a function and returns a modified function; syntactic sugar for func = decorator(func). Basic pattern: def my_decorator(func): def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): # before logic; result=func(*args,**kwargs); # after; return result; return wrapper. What @my_decorator does: say_hello=my_decorator(say_hello) at definition time. functools.wraps: use @functools.wraps(func) inside wrapper to preserve __name__, __doc__; without it, introspection breaks. Decorator with arguments: three levels of nesting: def repeat(n): def decorator(func): def wrapper(*args,**kwargs): [func(*args,**kwargs) for _ in range(n)]; return wrapper; return decorator. Class-based decorator: implement __init__(self,func) and __call__(self,*args,**kwargs); useful for stateful decorators. Common uses: @property; @staticmethod, @classmethod; @lru_cache(maxsize=128) for memoisation; @login_required; @retry; @timing for logging execution time."),
("Explain database transactions and ACID.","Transaction: unit of work fully completed or fully rolled back. ACID: (1) Atomicity -- all operations succeed or none do; implemented via write-ahead log (WAL): changes written to log before applied; on recovery, incomplete transactions rolled back. (2) Consistency -- transaction brings DB from one valid state to another; NOT NULL, UNIQUE, FOREIGN KEY constraints enforced at commit. (3) Isolation -- concurrent transactions do not see each other's incomplete writes; isolation levels: READ UNCOMMITTED (dirty read possible); READ COMMITTED (no dirty reads -- default PostgreSQL); REPEATABLE READ (same row reads give same result within transaction -- default MySQL); SERIALIZABLE (full isolation, highest consistency, lowest concurrency). Phenomena: dirty read (uncommitted data), non-repeatable read (row changes between reads), phantom read (new rows appear). (4) Durability -- committed transactions survive crashes; WAL flushed to persistent storage before COMMIT returns; fsync() ensures OS buffers written to disk."),
("What is the CAP theorem?","CAP theorem (Brewer, 2000): a distributed system can provide at most two of three guarantees simultaneously: (1) Consistency -- every read receives the most recent write or an error (all nodes see same data at same time); (2) Availability -- every request receives a response (not necessarily most recent data); (3) Partition Tolerance -- system continues operating despite network partitions (some nodes cannot communicate). In distributed systems, network partitions are inevitable, so you must choose between C and A when partition occurs. CP systems (Consistency + Partition Tolerance): choose to reject requests when partition occurs rather than return stale data; examples: ZooKeeper, HBase, Cassandra with strong consistency, etcd. AP systems (Availability + Partition Tolerance): continue serving requests during partition; return possibly stale data; eventually consistent; examples: Cassandra default, DynamoDB, CouchDB. CA systems (Consistency + Availability): only possible without partitions; traditional single-node RDBMS. PACELC extension: even without partition, tradeoff between latency and consistency; some AP systems tradeoff EL (Latency vs Consistency during normal operation)."),
("Explain the difference between processes and threads.","Process: independent program instance with its own memory space (code, data, heap, stack); isolated from other processes (memory protection); IPC (Inter-Process Communication) needed to share data: pipes, sockets, shared memory, message queues; process creation (fork/CreateProcess) is expensive; crash of one process does not crash others. Thread: unit of execution within a process; shares process address space (heap, globals); has own stack and registers; lightweight (creation cheaper than process); IPC is trivial (shared variables) but requires synchronisation (locks, mutexes, semaphores) to avoid race conditions. Race condition: two threads access/modify shared data without synchronisation; result depends on execution order (non-deterministic). Mutex (mutual exclusion): only one thread holds lock at a time; protects critical sections. Deadlock: thread A holds lock 1, waits for lock 2; thread B holds lock 2, waits for lock 1; both wait forever; prevention: always acquire locks in same order. Python GIL: CPython allows only one thread to run Python bytecode at a time; threads share process but not truly parallel for CPU-bound work; I/O releases GIL (threads useful for I/O-bound). Goroutines (Go): very lightweight cooperative threads; millions can exist; Go runtime multiplexes onto OS threads."),
("What are the most important Python standard library modules?","Key Python standard library modules: (1) os -- OS interaction: os.path.join(), os.listdir(), os.environ, os.getcwd(), os.makedirs(). (2) sys -- interpreter internals: sys.argv, sys.exit(), sys.path, sys.stdin/stdout/stderr, sys.getrecursionlimit(). (3) pathlib -- modern file paths: Path('dir') / 'file.txt'; path.exists(), path.read_text(), path.write_text(). (4) json -- JSON encode/decode: json.dumps(obj), json.loads(string), json.dump/load for files. (5) datetime -- date and time: datetime.now(), timedelta, strftime/strptime. (6) collections -- specialised containers: deque, Counter, defaultdict, OrderedDict, namedtuple. (7) itertools -- iterator tools: chain, islice, product, combinations, permutations, groupby. (8) functools -- higher-order functions: partial, reduce, lru_cache, wraps. (9) re -- regular expressions: re.findall, re.search, re.sub, re.compile. (10) subprocess -- run external commands: subprocess.run(['ls','-la'], capture_output=True, text=True). (11) threading, multiprocessing, asyncio -- concurrency. (12) logging -- structured logging with levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL). (13) unittest / pytest (third-party) -- testing. (14) csv -- CSV reading/writing. (15) sqlite3 -- built-in SQLite database. (16) hashlib -- cryptographic hashing: hashlib.sha256(data.encode()).hexdigest(). (17) socket -- low-level networking. (18) argparse -- command-line argument parsing."),
("Explain what makes code clean and maintainable.","Clean code principles: (1) Meaningful names -- variables, functions, classes named for what they do; getUserById not getUB; days_until_event not d; boolean names start with is/has/can: is_valid, has_permission. (2) Small functions -- function does one thing; fits on screen; if you need to write a comment explaining what a code block does, extract it into a named function. (3) DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) -- every piece of knowledge has a single representation; duplication leads to inconsistency when one copy is updated and another is not; extract repeated logic into functions or classes. (4) Comments explain why not what -- code shows what; comments explain business rules, non-obvious decisions, algorithmic choices that are not self-evident; avoid: # increment i by 1; i += 1. (5) Error handling -- handle errors explicitly; do not swallow exceptions silently; fail loudly and early; return typed errors or raise specific exceptions. (6) Consistency -- follow project/language conventions (PEP 8 for Python, Google Style Guide); use linters (flake8, pylint, ruff). (7) Testability -- write code that can be tested; dependency injection; avoid global state; pure functions (output depends only on input). (8) Single level of abstraction per function -- do not mix high-level orchestration with low-level detail in same function. (9) Boy Scout Rule -- leave code cleaner than you found it; refactor as you go. Code review and pair programming enforce these standards."),
]

existing = sum(1 for _ in OUT.open()) if OUT.exists() else 0
print(f"Existing pairs: {existing}")
print(f"New pairs to add: {len(pairs)}")

